content.text:
Missions mobile issues:
1. Bottom nav overlaps content; expected.
2. The header "Misiones." and button "Pedir misión" are side by side; the description text wraps awkwardly ("Los agentes los toman, los mueven y dejan la evidencia.") and button overlaps? Looks okay. The section top uses flex align-end with gap; at 360-390, the h1 36px and button to the right; the lede width is constrained to ~230px, causing 4 lines. Fine but a bit cramped. Could improve mobile by stacking the section-top (title then button) or making the button full width? The current layout is usable. The button "Pedir misión" sits at the right of the title/description, causing the description to be narrow. Same on agents page: "Cómo se unen" button squeezes the lede to ~230px. On mobile, better stack the action button below or place it aligned right under the text. The user asked mobile-first, so polish this.

Design option:
```
@media (max-width: 639px) {
  .section-top { flex-direction: column; align-items: flex-start; gap: 14px; }
  .section-top .primary-button, .section-top .ghost-button { align-self: stretch; justify-content: center; }
}
```
But that makes every view's action a full-width button, adding height. Alternatively align-self: flex-end. Or keep title full width and put button on its own line right-aligned. The lede then gets full width. I think full-width button on mobile is finger-friendly. But for "Plan" view, the "Ver misiones" button full width is okay. For "World" view, "Conexiones" full width maybe overkill. Hmm.

Alternative: make `.section-top` wrap, with the title block full width and the button below:
```
@media (max-width:639px){
 .section-top{flex-wrap:wrap;align-items:flex-start;}
 .section-top > div{flex:1 1 100%;}
 .section-top > .primary-button,.section-top > .ghost-button{flex:0 0 auto;align-self:flex-end;margin-top:-8px;}
}
```
But the description then spans full width, and the button goes below the description right-aligned. This adds a row. Better readability. However, for pages where the button is important, it's still visible.

Look at the agents page: the lede is squeezed into 4 lines by the button. On mobile-first, I'd prefer the action button below the lede. Let's implement wrap at max-width 639. Also the top padding. Need to make sure the button doesn't crowd.

CSS:
```
@media (max-width: 639px) {
  .section-top { flex-wrap: wrap; align-items: flex-start; }
  .section-top > div:first-child { flex: 1 1 100%; }
  .section-top > .primary-button, .section-top > .ghost-button { align-self: flex-start; margin-top: -4px; }
}
```
But this makes the button left-aligned below, which looks intentional. Or align-self: flex-end for right alignment. I'd go with `align-self: flex-end` to keep it near the title's right edge. The button below the lede on the right. That uses space and keeps thumb reach. But full-width might be better for tap. 36px height is fine. Let's use `align-self: flex-end`.

Also the "Home" greeting already stacks.

Let's also check the agents list: agent cards 2 columns at 390, each ~174px wide. Text is small but readable. The agent portrait 54px. Fine. The agent "last action" text is 8px—too small? It appears ~10px in the screenshot due to scaling, but CSS 8px. On a real phone, 8px is tiny. The app overall uses very small font sizes (8-10px) in many places, which was the design choice for a dense dashboard. Mobile-first should prioritize readability; 8px is below accessibility guidelines (12px minimum). This is a concern. The whole UI uses small fonts (eyebrows 9px, meta 7.5px). The screenshots at 390px are readable because we're viewing a 390px image; on a phone the physical size is small. But the design is intentionally editorial/dense. Should we increase key text sizes? The main titles are large. Meta text at 7.5px is very small. For a state viewer, the important text (mission titles 14px, agent names 14px, last action 8px) is somewhat small. We could bump key body text:
- `.agent-last` 8px → 9.5px
- `.now-action` 10px → 10.5
- `.issue-title` 10px → 11
- `.mission-desc` 9px → 9.5
- `.lede` 13px → 13.5.
But this could break layouts. The screenshot looks crisp at 390; 8px on a 390 CSS px width with DPR 3 is physically ~2.1mm, small. However, many dashboards use 10-11px for meta. The user didn't ask for accessibility. Still, mobile-first implies legibility.

Given the time, I'd make targeted increases:
- `.agent-last` font-size: 9px, min-height 24.
- `.agent-role` 7.5→8.5
- `.agent-repo` 7.5→8.5
- `.agent-hb` 6.5→7.5
- `.issue-title` 10→11
- `.issue-meta` 7.5→8
- `.now-action` 10→10.5
- `.mission-desc` 9→9.5
- `.mission-meta` etc. small.
But the design is consistent with tiny mono labels. I'd avoid broad changes at this late stage, since it could cause overflow. The main content (titles) is legible; meta is secondary. Fine.

Now the section-top wrap is a bigger layout improvement. Let's implement and screenshot.

Also mobile agents page: the "Cómo se unen" button floating right of the lede is awkward. With wrap, it will go below the lede, aligned right. Good.

Missions page: same.

Worldpage: "Conexiones" button below the lede, right aligned. Fine.
Plan page: "Ver misiones" button below. Fine.

At mobile, the section-top padding bottom 23; with button below add maybe 16. Use gap 12.

Let's patch CSS max-width 639:
```
.section-top { flex-wrap: wrap; align-items: flex-start; }
.section-top > div:first-child { flex: 1 1 100%; }
.section-top > .primary-button, .section-top > .ghost-button { align-self: flex-end; margin-top: -6px; }
```
But `.section-top` on mobile has h1 and lede; full width. Fine.

Wait the `div` selector may also target the button? Buttons are direct children, not div. Good.

Bump CSS v14.

